<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sudetes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sudeten Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sudetes are a mountain range of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> along the border of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, with a small part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, stretching about 300 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-gate">Moravian Gate</a>. The highest peak is Sněžka in the Giant Mountains. The range encloses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klodzko">Kłodzko</a> basin and later gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a>, the German-speaking borderlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sudetes.jpg" alt="Sudetes" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcin Drozd · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%9Anie%C5%BCne_Kot%C5%82y_-_panoramio_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
